lesson g1-bonds-to-ten v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Bonds to ten"
  goal "Know the pairs that make ten, see them as two rods against a ten, and find a part you cannot see."

  look {
    scene 30x11 {
      rods stair rods=[10, 6] mode=stair numbers=true at=canvas(1, 1)
      numberbond bond whole=10 parts=[6, 4] right-of=stair gap=3
      note n "Six and four. The piece left over is the answer." width=13 below=stair gap=1
    }
    say """
      Lay a rod against the ten rod and the pair is there to see: the rod, and the piece that fills
      the gap.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice bonds.rods count=1 seed=4
    practice bonds.missing-part count=2 seed=6
    practice groups.eggbox count=1 seed=6
    practice bonds.under-the-cup count=1 seed=3
    practice bonds.balloons-away count=1 seed=2
    practice bonds.sticker-start count=1 seed=5
    practice bonds.wrong-bond count=1 seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show bonds.three-parts p=3 q=2 easy-p=4 easy-q=3 hard-p=1 hard-q=2
    show bonds.level-the-plank n=10 a=3 easy-n=7 hard-a=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show bonds.two-cups d=4 easy-d=2 hard-d=8
  }
  remember {
    say "Pairs that make ten: 1 and 9, 2 and 8, 3 and 7, 4 and 6, 5 and 5."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Say the pair both ways round, six and four, four and six, so the child learns one fact instead
    of two. The counters under the cup cannot be counted, which is the point: the child has to use
    the pair. The balloons and the sticker chart put the missing part in the middle and at the start
    of a story, and the wrong bond asks the child to check every pair rather than make one. In the
    three-star question any pair that makes ten is a fine first guess; what matters is adjusting it
    until the two numbers are the right distance apart.

    The see-saw is answered by standing weights on step 3. Props hold the plank level until the
    child presses Check, so the pair has to be worked out rather than watched for. On paper the
    child writes the two weights in the box over the step.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the parts to find are one to five, the plank's load is seven, and the two cups are two apart."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the parts to find are six or more, the egg box has most of its cups empty, and the two cups are eight apart."
  }
}
